F205 — a file-scope enum constant hidden by a contract member

    enum Kind { Helper = 3, Solo = 4 };
    struct C : ContractBase {
        PRIVATE_FUNCTION(Helper) { ... }
        ...  state.mut().hidden = Helper;     // clang: error. TypeScript: stores 3.
    }

Name lookup inside a member function searches class scope before namespace scope, so `Helper` names the
member function and assigning it to a `uint64` is ill-formed. clang says so; the TypeScript backend took
the enum constant and compiled the contract. `Solo` is the control — nothing in class scope hides it —
and both backends store 4 for it.

The identifier path now checks class scope first. `hasStateParam` is the boundary: it marks the
contract's own entries, so a qpi.h body that happens to use a constant sharing a name with some
contract's entry is unaffected.

Measured: both backends refuse EnumHidden, which is what clang does. Suite 1085/0.

